::callout{type="warning"}
The drag handle is the feature's main entry point, so every drag-reorder writes the wrong data to the parent form. The snapshot tests do not exercise `handleDragEnd`, so this passes CI.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="141-143"}
if (oldIndex !== -1 && newIndex !== -1) {
  moveItem(oldIndex, newIndex);
}

Prefix items are protected only by `useSortable({ disabled: !canMove })` on the row. The `SortableContext` still lists every key, including the prefix keys at indices `< prefixItemsCount` (lines 161-164). `handleDragEnd` (lines 130-145) then moves the dragged item to whatever index `over` resolves to, with no check that the destination is outside the prefix range. A regular item can therefore be dropped into position 0 of a `prefixItems` tuple, which displaces a fixed-position prefix item and breaks the schema's per-index typing. I haven't confirmed whether dnd-kit reports disabled sortables as `over`. Either way, `handleDragEnd` should not rely on that behavior.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="133-143"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR says prefix items "remain fixed in position", and this path breaks that promise. Adding a guard in the drop handler fixes it regardless of dnd-kit's disabled-droppable behavior.
